# Standard HQL library

The declarations HQL ships with live in [`std/`](../../../std/README.md), written
in HQL rather than described in prose. One file per module, mirroring the
extension split: `core`, `doc`, `graph`, `knowledge`, `retrieval`.

These cards and those files say the same thing twice, which is a real cost worth
naming. The division is that **the file is the declaration and the card is the
argument**. `std/graph.hql` states that `Link<S, T> <: Edge<S, T>`;
[[graph-type]] explains why an edge is the primitive and why its payload is
[[data-type|Data]]. When the two disagree, the card is describing something that
no longer exists, and the card is what gets fixed.

`std/` is deliberately not [the corpus](../../../examples/README.md). The corpus
records what was considered and may keep a withdrawn spelling beside a current
one; a standard library may not. There is one declaration of `Edge`, and it is
the one that is true now.

## Not yet loadable

The parser has no `type` statement, so nothing in `std/` is read by the binary
or checked by a test. Tracked as
[issue 0003](../../issues/0003-type-declarations.md), which is itself blocked on
the angle-bracket migration recorded as `INC-24`.

A standard library is a bootstrap non-goal in
[requirements](../../models/requirements/bootstrap.md), and that still holds:
having a home for the declarations is not the same as shipping a library of
functions.

See [[type-system]] for `<:`, generics and refinement, [[hql/core]] for the
vocabulary, and [[hql/extensions]] for the modules the layout follows.
